wildos-winmode (0.3.8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Windows stays lean: guest/tune.ps1 holds the machine-wide policies,
    services, scheduled tasks, apps and features Windows mode does
    without, and a start-up task inside Windows runs it at every boot,
    because Office and Windows updates were found switching them back.
    After each Windows update the same run clears the components the
    update replaced. New: Windows' AI fabric, inventory and link tracking
    services, Recall, widgets, Phone Link, Quick Assist and a few
    capabilities, reserved storage and restore points; services grouped
    as on smaller PCs. setup.ps1 hands its machine-wide part to it.
    New setups only: a Windows set up before it is left as it is, since
    the clean-up removes apps and features its owner may use. Guest
    upkeep delivers newer versions only to a Windows setup gave it to
    (state "tune"). Measured on build 26300: 22.4 GB to 15.5 GB on disk,
    and a cumulative update installed on top without errors and left it
    all in place. A fresh setup with Microsoft 365 came to 15.6 GB.
  * Outlook search works in a new setup with Microsoft 365: setup cleans
    up before Office is installed, so Windows Search was left disabled.
    The start-up run now turns it back on (delayed start) once Outlook
    is there.
  * Lifecycle events reach winmoded: "virsh event --all --event
    lifecycle" is refused (the two options are mutually exclusive), so
    the watcher exited at once and only launches and wakes ever noticed
    a change. A Windows that had just been set up never went idle, and
    one that stopped by itself went unnoticed. The 30 s check now also
    catches any change an event missed.
  * An idle Windows is trimmed to what it uses plus 512 MB, no lower than
    1.5 GB (was plus 1.5 GB, no lower than 3 GB), and once more just
    before it pauses or saves. Measured on Debian 13's libvirt, Windows
    barely pages there and every MB comes back to Wild OS: about 1 GB
    more for the desktop while Windows waits, and a smaller save.
  * Automatic saves a paused Windows to disk once it has been paused for 15
    minutes, plugged in too. Measured with the trim: a save takes under
    3 s and Windows is back to Remote Desktop answering in about 5 s
    (from paused, under 1 s), while paused it held about 2 GB. Short
    breaks still come back at once; long ones give all its memory back.
  * The guest upkeep reads a script's result from its output only, so
    PowerShell warnings are no longer taken for it, and gives tune.ps1 up
    to 30 minutes.

